        "title": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e4c8e167-221b-5005-acc4-46a539f68550",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\frac{x}{2}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nFactor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}=2\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{x}{2})^2}.\n$$\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2=3$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=3\\left(\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
